WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.660
After being on the air for nearly 30 years
and more than 600 episodes, there really aren't

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.000
many aspects of life that haven't been touched
upon by The Simpsons.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.170
As surreal as their animated adventures may
be, from time to time, life imitates art.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:18.220
We've lost count of the times that bizarre
storylines from Springfield actually came

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:24.230
true, but here are just a few occasions when
The Simpsons freakishly predicted the future.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.230
President Trump

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.950
One of many episodes that showed an alternate-reality
future for Springfield's first family, "Bart

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:35.020
to the Future" aired in the year 2000, and
we learn Lisa has just been elected President

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.550
of the United States.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:40.560
During her first cabinet meeting, President
Simpson mentions a familiar name.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.650
"As you know, we've inherited quite a budget
crunch from President Trump."

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.440
We all know how the future turned out.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.430
It's worth mentioning that this particular
episode was written during Donald Trump's

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:55.350
failed presidential run in the year 2000,
and the writers themselves saw the possibility

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:59.630
of a Trump presidency as the most ridiculous
thing they could imagine for the future…

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:02.470
which can only mean that Lisa Simpson is up
next.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:03.470
And then?

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:04.470
President Kang.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:08.260
"All hail President Kang!"

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:09.399
Could be worse.

00:01:09.399 --> 00:01:13.689
We could be eating Soylent, which is literally
named after ground-up people from a sci-fi

00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:15.570
movie starring Charlton Heston.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:16.570
Wait…

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.869
"Soylent is food.

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:23.600
While not intended to replace every meal,
Soylent can replace any meal."

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:25.640
"Blech!

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.140
Oh, what a bleak, horrible future we live
in!"

00:01:28.149 --> 00:01:29.549
All you can eat

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:34.680
In 1992's "New Kid on the Block," Homer sees
a TV commercial for an all-you-can-eat seafood

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.069
restaurant called the Frying Dutchman.

00:01:37.069 --> 00:01:41.359
Homer proceeds to eat until closing time,
and gets kicked out…while still hungry.

00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:46.909
He sues the restaurant for false advertising,
but Homer and the Sea Captain come to an agreement.

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:51.729
"Come see Bottomless Pete, nature's cruelest
mistake.

00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:54.479
Come for the freak, stay for the food!"

00:01:54.479 --> 00:01:59.859
In 2012, Bill Wisth went to a Wisconsin restaurant
called Chuck's Place for its weekly Friday

00:01:59.859 --> 00:02:01.729
night fish fry buffet.

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:05.869
After a few trips to the restaurant — and
racking up a running tab — he wanted more,

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:08.489
but the restaurant claimed they'd run out.

00:02:08.489 --> 00:02:09.970
That's when Wisth refused to pay.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:14.220
"We asked for more fish and they refused to
give us any more fish."

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.970
The owner of Chuck's Place claimed Wisth had
eaten more than 20 pieces of fish…and was

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:21.510
violating the buffet's "no sharing" policy.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.299
He came back, with a vengeance.

00:02:23.299 --> 00:02:25.269
"And he wasn't done.

00:02:25.269 --> 00:02:28.310
He came back two days later, with a picket
sign."

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:33.700
Wisth planned on picketing every day, but
by early 2013, he'd already moved on to other

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:34.700
protests.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:35.700
But really…

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:40.329
"Did these sound like the actions of a man
who had ALL he could eat?"

00:02:40.329 --> 00:02:41.329
"No!"

00:02:41.329 --> 00:02:43.530
"That could have been me!"

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.650
People unknowingly ate gym mats

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:50.360
Over the course of the series, the secrets
of Springfield Elementary's lunchroom cost-cutting

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.260
have been explored again and again, but even
Lunchlady Doris has her limits.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:55.950
"Where do you want these beef hearts?"

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.349
"On the floor."

00:02:57.349 --> 00:03:02.950
In the 1995 episode "The PTA Disbands," even
Doris objects to using shredded newspaper

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:05.219
and old gym mats as ingredients.

00:03:05.219 --> 00:03:06.219
"Posh!

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:09.099
Shredded newspapers add much needed roughage
and essential inks.

00:03:09.099 --> 00:03:11.879
Besides, they didn't notice the old gym mats."

00:03:11.879 --> 00:03:14.930
"There's very little meat in these gym mats."

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:20.469
In 2014, a minor but disgusting scandal developed
when the Environmental Working Group released

00:03:20.469 --> 00:03:27.250
a report that found a compound called azodicarbonamide
in around 500 processed food products, primarily

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:32.249
breads, including Pillsbury Dinner Rolls,
your favorite five dollar footlongs, and Wonder

00:03:32.249 --> 00:03:33.249
Bread.

00:03:33.249 --> 00:03:37.249
So, if you've ever wondered what put the "wonder"
in Wonder Bread… it's yoga mats, and yoga

00:03:37.249 --> 00:03:38.740
is totally healthy.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.349
The baby translator

00:03:40.349 --> 00:03:45.219
By Season Two, Homer had met his long-lost
brother Herb, but he leaves Herb in financial

00:03:45.219 --> 00:03:50.549
ruin after he bets his whole car manufacturing
business on a ridiculous car Homer designs.

00:03:50.549 --> 00:03:53.069
Which was called "The Homer," obviously.

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:54.359
"Any questions?"

00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:59.810
Left destitute, he moves in with the Simpsons,
where he creates a baby translator that turns

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:01.709
an infant's noises into English.

00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:02.709
"Maggie.

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:03.700
Maggie!"

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.000
"Where did you go?"

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.000
"Peekaboo!"

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:09.599
"Oh, there you are.

00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:10.599
Very amusing."

00:04:10.599 --> 00:04:16.610
The episode originally aired in 1992, and
by the year 2004, Japanese tech and toy company

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:21.830
Takara developed a real-life prototype of
something very similar, after previously selling

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:24.640
a device that analyzed the sounds of dogs
barking.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:29.910
The prototype analyzed a baby's sounds, facial
expressions, and body temperature to alert

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:33.480
parents if the baby was hungry, tired, or
needed to be changed.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:38.000
While the final product never emerged, other
companies took the reins and produced other

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.250
devices to analyze the fleshy, wet, loud riddles
that are infants, ensuring that you'll always

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:47.100
be able to completely emotionally detach from
your helpless offspring.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.190
A donut-shaped universe

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:55.450
At the end of "They Saved Lisa's Brain," a
1999 episode in which Lisa and her local MENSA

00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:00.250
chapter attempt to rule Springfield with their
superior intellect, the smartest guy in the

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:04.570
world makes a cameo: physicist and cosmologist
Stephen Hawking.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.520
And he has some interesting ideas.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:10.550
"Your theory of a donut-shaped universe is
intriguing, Homer.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:13.430
I may have to steal it."

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:14.430
"Wow."

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:15.690
Technically, the shape of a donut is a torus.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:21.420
And yes, as it turns out, the universe may
actually be shaped like the delicious confection.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:26.240
Four years after Homer Simpson "proposed"
the theory, cosmologists noticed radiation

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:31.190
patterns in space suggesting that the universe
was round and tube-like, but "empty" in the

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:32.190
middle.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:35.050
And that's not the only time Homer's been
the smartest man in Springfield.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:38.220
"I am so smart!

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:39.860
I am so smart!

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:40.980
S-M-R-T!

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.680
I mean, S-M-A-R-T!"

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:44.020
The Higgs boson

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:49.970
While first proposed in 1964, it wasn't until
2013 that physicists discovered the Higgs

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:53.340
boson, the field that basically holds atoms
together.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:57.960
In other words, the discovery helps to define
the very fabric of existence, and helps to

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.580
explain how the universe and matter work.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.780
And once again, Homer was on the cutting edge
of science.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:08.760
In the 1998 episode "The Wizard of Evergreen
Terrace," Homer attempts to become a scientist

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.940
and inventor, and is seen messing around with
some formulas on a chalkboard.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:16.630
His meaningless math actually gets pretty
close to the the actual molecular weight of

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:21.190
the Higgs particle, which, out of an infinite
possible equations, is pretty impressive.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:25.790
But more importantly, when is reality going
to finally catch up with the toilet chair?

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:29.220
"Believe me, every man in America will want
to have one."

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:33.000
"Gang way, gotta poop!"

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.000
Lady Gaga

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:39.950
The halftime show of the 2017 Super Bowl made
headlines, with Lady Gaga delivering a performance

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:46.250
that was absolutely surreal, featuring high-wire
stunts, pyrotechnics, and theatrical costumes.

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:50.990
Having provided her own voice for her 2012
appearance on The Simpsons, Gaga was definitely

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:56.090
familiar with her earlier animated performance,
which bore some striking similarities to her

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:57.540
Super Bowl show.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:02.370
Granted, Gaga's Springfield self was only
a slight exaggeration on her normally outlandish

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:03.370
persona…

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:04.370
"It's me, Gaga!

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:09.980
Why is everyone looking at my purple hair
full of bluebirds?"

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:12.080
...but this one is pretty spot-on.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:14.000
The Albuquerque Isotopes

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:19.280
The 2001 episode "Hungry, Hungry Homer" is
all about Homer going on a hunger strike to

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.300
prevent the local minor league baseball team,
the Springfield Isotopes, from secretly moving

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:25.310
to Albuquerque.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:29.130
"Dancing away my hunger pangs.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:32.020
Moving my feet so my stomach won't hurt."

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:37.920
In 2002, the minor league Calgary Cannons
announced a move to Albuquerque, and the local

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:42.030
paper published a poll asking readers to come
up with a new name for the team.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:47.360
No strangers to pop culture, the name "Isotopes"
won with 67 percent of the vote.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.650
While the idea very clearly came from The
Simpsons, "Isotopes" is appropriate for a

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:54.890
team moving so close to the Los Alamos National
Laboratory.

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:59.860
At least the power plants in New Mexico can't
possibly be as bad as in Springfield, right?

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:04.661
"Well, in Southern New Mexico, federal officials
have confirmed that a local nuclear power

00:08:04.661 --> 00:08:08.560
plant is leaking waste and releasing radiation
into the surrounding area."

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:09.560
Nope.

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:10.560
Those suck too.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:11.560
Stay safe, Isotopes.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:13.340
Thanks for watching!

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:16.120
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:18.450
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

